{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_2338494947#9_3133992953","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly mentions requirements for humans on Mars—habitat, supplies, radiation protection, and fail-safe life support—but it is framed as an answer to why people have not gone to Mars, not a document dedicated to how to support life on other planets.","learned":"Supporting people on Mars requires an artificial environment including habitats, transport/surface access, radiation protection, supplies, and reliable life-support systems."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ nasa esa) \"human life\" mars (+ habitat habitats) (+ oxygen water food) (+ radiation \"life support\"))","reason":"Search for authoritative space-agency material specifically describing systems needed to support human life on Mars."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ nasa esa) \"human life\" mars (+ habitat habitats) (+ oxygen water food) (+ radiation \"life support\"))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_755607027#4_1457429319","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3712","rank":1,"snippet":"<cover>water-filled skylight and intermittent windows floods the interior while still keeping the crew safe from harmful solar and cosmic radiation,” according to AI SpaceFactory. The pods would be manufactured using “an innovative mixture of basalt fiber extracted from Martian rock and renewable bioplastic (polylactic acid, or PLA) processed from plants grown on Mars,” AI SpaceFactory says on the agency’s website, eliminating the problematic issue of interplanetary transport of materials. Humans may still be several years away from even transporting people to Mars (SpaceX’s Elon Musk has claimed he can make it happen by 2024, though NASA is shooting for the 2030s ). But NASA launched its design competition in 2015, asking architects and technology experts to design habitats suitable for humans to live in on Mars. The designs had to be able to be 3D-printed using sustainable materials found on Mars, as NASA wants to use Mars rovers outfitted with large-scale 3D printers to build habitats before humans even arrive. AI SpaceFactory was awarded $500,000 for its winning prototype, which beat out 60 different teams (a team from Penn State University finished in second place, winning $200,000). AI SpaceFactory’s biopolymer composite of basalt </cover>"}}
