{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1389117679#9_2974791692","relevance":3,"reason":"This NASA page is organized around real technologies for living/traveling to Mars, including habitat, plant farm, water recovery, oxygen generation, and spacesuits, and the snippet directly explains ECLSS water recycling for human life support.","learned":"NASA life support uses Environmental Control and Life Support Systems to recover and recycle water from urine, sweat, hygiene, and other sources so it can be filtered and reused for drinking."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3721","reason":"The NASA ECLSS query produced strong grade-3 evidence; continue to find more authoritative NASA results on oxygen, water, waste, and food systems for Mars/deep-space life support."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3721"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#0_2973295301","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3721","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#0_2973295301\" , \"end_char\": 1164.000000 , \"headings\": \"Life Support Systems Life Support Systems Sustaining Humans Beyond Earth SYSTEMS ARCHITECTURE ENVIRONMENTAL MONITORING ATMOSPHERE MANAGEMENT Oxygen Generation and Recovery Carbon Dioxide Removal Trace Contaminant and Particulate Control WATER MANAGEMENT ADDITIONAL RESOURCES \" , \"segment\": \"Life Support Systems | NASA Life Support Systems Sustaining Humans Beyond Earth<cover>Environmental Control and Life Support Overview diagram that includes three components: Environmental Monitoring, Atmosphere Management, and Water Management. Credits: NASA Life Support systems are an integral part of a deep space </cover>habitation capability, which is one of the main focus areas of AES. Habitation systems will provide a safe place for humans as we travel deeper into space, and life support is key to survival inside a deep space habitat. The Simplified Life Support Systems Schematic shows all of the elements of a life support system, including various processors for waste, air, and water. Credits: NASA NASA’s Life Support Systems (LSS) activities develop the capabilities to sustain humans who are living and working in space - away from Earth’"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"14/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#0_2973295301","relevance":3,"reason":"This NASA page is directly dedicated to life support systems for sustaining humans beyond Earth and explains that deep-space habitats need environmental monitoring, atmosphere management, water management, and processors for waste, air, and water.","learned":"NASA identifies life support as essential to deep-space habitation, with systems for environmental monitoring, atmosphere management, water management, and processing waste, air, and water."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3721","reason":"The NASA ECLSS query is yielding high-quality dedicated life-support evidence, so continue to collect more NASA documents on sustaining humans beyond Earth."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3721"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1389117679#8_2974790013","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3721","rank":3,"snippet":"of crops to help meet the nutritional needs of future astronauts on Mars. Water Recovery There are no lakes, river or oceans on the surface of Mars, and sending water from Earth would take more than nine months. Astronauts on Mars must be able to create their own water supply. The Ares 3 crew does not waste a drop on <cover>Mars with their water reclaimer, and Watney needs to use his ingenuity to come up with some peculiar ways to stay hydrated and ensure his survival on the Red Planet. On the International Space Station, no drop of sweat, tears, or even urine goes to waste. The Environmental Control and Life Support System recovers and recycles water from everywhere: urine, hand washing, oral hygiene, and other sources. Through the Water Recovery System (WRS), water is reclaimed and filtered, ready for consumption. One astronaut simply put it, “Yesterday’s coffee turns into tomorrow’s coffee.”\" , \"start_char\": 4455.000000 , \"title\": \"Nine Real NASA </cover>Technologies in 'The Martian' | NASA\" , \"url\": \"https://www.nasa.gov/feature/nine-real-nasa-technologies-in-the-martian/\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"15/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1389117679#8_2974790013","relevance":3,"reason":"This NASA page segment is directly relevant to supporting life on Mars, explaining that astronauts must create their own water supply and that ECLSS recovers and recycles sweat, urine, hygiene water, and other sources into drinkable water.","learned":"Mars life support must include water recovery/recycling because transporting water from Earth is impractical; NASA systems reclaim and filter wastewater into potable water."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3721","reason":"Continue the productive NASA ECLSS query to find additional non-duplicate authoritative evidence on air, water, waste, and food systems for sustaining humans beyond Earth."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3721"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_314704254#7_358727438","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3721","rank":4,"snippet":"science. So, how does a mission to Mars play out? Journey to Mars begins on International Space Station Right now, some 250 miles above land on the International Space Station, American astronaut Scott Kelly and Russian cosmonaut Mikhail Kornienko are studying the effect of long periods without gravity on the human body. The “ One Year Mission ” on the space station will help prepare for future journeys. Kelly led a Twitter chat Saturday, six months into his mission, sharing insights into what they’ve learned so far. No drop of sweat, coffee or urine goes to waste on the station thanks to the <cover>Environmental Control and Life Support System. The system recovers and recycles water from various sources so it can be reclaimed and filtered for consumption through the Water Recovery System. The International Space Station uses a deployable fresh-food production system known as Veggie to build a continuous source of food.\" , \"start_char\": 3190.000000 , \"title\": \"What 'The Martian' can teach us | CNN\" , \"url\": \"http://www.cnn.com/2015/09/19/entertainment/matt-damon-mars-nasa-</cover>feat/index.html\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"16/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_314704254#7_358727438","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage provides relevant evidence that ECLSS recycles water for consumption and Veggie can provide fresh food, but it is a CNN article about lessons from The Martian rather than a document dedicated to how to support life on other planets.","learned":"Key support systems for Mars/deep-space missions include water recovery through ECLSS and fresh-food production systems such as Veggie."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nasa \"life support systems\" \"sustaining humans beyond earth\" (+ oxygen water waste atmosphere))","reason":"Search the exact NASA page title already identified as highly relevant, to retrieve more segments from a dedicated authoritative life-support document."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nasa \"life support systems\" \"sustaining humans beyond earth\" (+ oxygen water waste atmosphere))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#11_2973315338","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3722","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#11_2973315338\" , \"end_char\": 8287.000000 , \"headings\": \"Life Support Systems<cover>Life Support Systems Sustaining Humans Beyond Earth SYSTEMS ARCHITECTURE ENVIRONMENTAL MONITORING ATMOSPHERE MANAGEMENT Oxygen Generation and Recovery Carbon Dioxide Removal Trace Contaminant and Particulate Control WATER MANAGEMENT ADDITIONAL RESOURCES \" , \"segment\": \"Trace Contaminant and Particulate Control In collaboration with the National Research Council's Committee on Toxicology, NASA </cover>has established guidelines for safe and acceptable levels of individual contaminants in spacecraft air and drinking water. A spacecraft cabin trace contaminant and particulate control system keeps the environment below the spacecraft’s maximum allowable concentration for chemicals and particulates. Particulates are small particles of matter like dust and aerosols. Both passive (filters) and active (scrubbers) methods contribute to the overall trace contaminant and particulate control system design. Work to advance technology and capability in this area under is focused on making improvements to current space station systems to improve performance and reduce consumables. Specific tasks include: Developing new technologies to control trace contamination. Testing technologies to monitor aerosols in spacecraft. Developing filtration ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#0_2973295301","rank":1,"relevance":3}]}}
