{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#1_2973296989","relevance":3,"reason":"This segment is from NASA's dedicated 'Life Support Systems: Sustaining Humans Beyond Earth' page and directly states that life-support systems sustain humans away from Earth's atmosphere and resources by managing waste, air, water, atmospheric pressure, oxygen levels, water supply, fire detection, and suppression.","learned":"NASA frames off-Earth life support as replacing Earth's protective atmosphere and resources through processors and monitoring for waste, air, water, atmospheric pressure, oxygen, water supply, and fire safety."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3722","reason":"The NASA life-support page continues to provide dedicated grade-3 evidence; continue retrieving more segments, especially oxygen generation and atmosphere-management details."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3722"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#2_2973298936","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3722","rank":7,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#2_2973298936\" , \"end_char\": 2509.000000 , \"headings\": \"Life Support Systems Life Support Systems<cover>Sustaining Humans Beyond Earth SYSTEMS ARCHITECTURE ENVIRONMENTAL MONITORING ATMOSPHERE MANAGEMENT Oxygen Generation and Recovery Carbon Dioxide Removal Trace Contaminant and Particulate Control WATER MANAGEMENT ADDITIONAL RESOURCES \" , \"segment\": \"Sending life-sustaining supplies 250 miles to the International Space Station (ISS) requires careful planning, and a robust international supply chain for delivery in one or two days after launch. Existing life support systems on the ISS provide oxygen, absorb carbon dioxide, and manage vaporous emissions from the astronauts themselves. Analysis of these systems allows NASA </cover>to identify areas where additional technology development is needed. Addressing any gaps will make life support systems more reliable and effective, which will lead to integrated testing on Earth and ISS in preparation for future human spaceflight missions deeper into the solar system. In the 2020s, humans will conduct proving ground missions in cislunar space, which lies between Earth and the Moon or its orbit. Demonstrating key capabilities like these advanced life support systems "}}
