{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#4_2973302470","relevance":3,"reason":"This segment is from NASA's dedicated 'Life Support Systems: Sustaining Humans Beyond Earth' page and directly explains life-support system integration, testing, reliability, maintenance, crew-time requirements, and environmental monitoring needed to keep enclosed spacecraft habitats safe.","learned":"Supporting humans beyond Earth requires integrated life-support architecture, testing, reliability and maintenance planning, and environmental monitoring because failures in enclosed habitats can make them unsafe."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nasa mars (+ oxygen \"oxygen generation\" moxie) (+ \"carbon dioxide\" atmosphere) (+ astronauts humans))","reason":"Diversify beyond general ECLSS pages toward NASA evidence on producing oxygen from the Martian atmosphere, a key method for supporting human life on another planet."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nasa mars (+ oxygen \"oxygen generation\" moxie) (+ \"carbon dioxide\" atmosphere) (+ astronauts humans))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_937424703#2_2027500616","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3723","rank":1,"snippet":"to keep an astronaut healthy for 10 minutes - but it's proof the technology works on the red planet. That's good news for the prospect of sending human explorers to Mars. Oxygen takes up a lot of room on a spacecraft, and it's unlikely that astronauts will be able to bring enough with them to Mars. So they'll need to produce their own oxygen from the Martian atmosphere, both for breathing and for fueling rockets to return to Earth. © JPL/NASA An artist's concept of <cover>astronauts and human habitats on Mars. JPL/NASA \"This is a critical first step at converting carbon dioxide to oxygen </cover>on Mars,\" Jim Reuter, the associate administrator for NASA's Space Technology Mission Directorate, said in a Wednesday press release. Load Error \"MOXIE has more work to do, but the results from this technology demonstration are full of promise as we move toward our goal of one day seeing humans on Mars,\" he added. \"\" , \"start_char\": 851.000000 , \"title\": \"NASA's Perseverance rover just turned CO2 into oxygen. The technology could help astronauts breathe on Mars.\" ,"}}
