{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_937424703#3_2027502579","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another direct segment of the MOXIE article, explaining that astronauts on Mars cannot bring enough oxygen and must produce oxygen from the Martian atmosphere by converting carbon dioxide, for both breathing and rocket propellant.","learned":"Mars life support and return capability can be supported by producing oxygen locally from atmospheric carbon dioxide, reducing the amount that must be launched from Earth."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nasa moxie mars \"oxygen\" \"carbon dioxide\" (+ breathe breathing propellant astronauts))","reason":"Search the exact MOXIE concept with NASA and key terms to find authoritative NASA/JPL pages about in-situ oxygen production for astronauts on Mars."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nasa moxie mars \"oxygen\" \"carbon dioxide\" (+ breathe breathing propellant astronauts))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_937424703#1_2027498869","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3724","rank":1,"snippet":"\": \"NASA's Perseverance rover just turned CO2 into oxygen. The technology could help astronauts breathe on Mars. NASA's Perseverance rover just turned CO2 into oxygen. The technology could help astronauts breathe on Mars. How MOXIE pulls oxygen out of thin air \" , \"segment\": \"Musk U-Turn Slams Bitcoin © NASA/JPL-Caltech Technicians in the cleanroom at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory lowering the MOXIE instrument into the belly of the Perseverance rover. NASA/JPL-Caltech<cover>NASA's Perseverance rover carried an oxygen-producing experiment called MOXIE to Mars. MOXIE on Tuesday converted carbon dioxide from Mars' atmosphere into oxygen for the first time. Any astronauts </cover>who visit Mars will need to produce their own oxygen to breathe and fuel rockets. See more stories on Insider's business page. NASA sent the Perseverance rover to Mars with some bonus technology: a device that can turn carbon dioxide into oxygen, much as trees do on Earth. The device, called the Mars Oxygen In-Situ Resource Utilization Experiment, or MOXIE, pulled carbon dioxide from the Martian atmosphere to produce its first oxygen on Tuesday. It's a small amount - 5.4 grams, enough "}}
