{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1390682938#0_2977560055","relevance":3,"reason":"This NASA page is directly dedicated to space farming for long-duration missions and explains how growing plants can support astronauts on the Moon or Mars by providing food, removing carbon dioxide, producing oxygen, and forming a bioregenerative life-support system.","learned":"Bioregenerative life support using plants can reduce resupply needs by producing food, removing toxic carbon dioxide, and generating oxygen for future Moon/Mars colonies and long-duration missions."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3725","reason":"The NASA farming query is producing dedicated grade-3 evidence on biological life support and food production, so continue to retrieve more relevant segments from this or similar documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3725"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1390682938#1_2977561793","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3725","rank":4,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1390682938#1_2977561793\" , \"end_char\": 1895.000000 , \"headings\": \" Feature Farming for the Future \" , \"segment\": \"Radishes are one plant species researchers are studying for possible use as a <cover>food crop on long-duration missions. Credit: NASA/KSC Since the Space Shuttle and even International Space Station expeditions are relatively short-duration endeavors, astronauts do well with physical and chemical forms of life support. But for future long-duration missions and colonies on the Moon or Mars, </cover>scientists believe a life support system with a biological component (such as plants) -- called a \"bioregenerative life support system\" -- has several benefits. \"If you continually resupply and deliver commodities like food, that will become much more costly than producing your own food,\" says Ray Wheeler, plant physiologist at Kennedy Space Center's Space Life Sciences Lab. \" You can achieve some autonomy with bioregenerative capability.\" But developing such a system isn't as simple as planting some fruits, vegetables and wheat in space or on distant planets. \"It's not a quick-start kind of thing,\" Wheeler "}}
