{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_118538044#1_201196668","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage supports one part of the need by explaining that crops grown in space or on other planets could help astronauts sustain themselves on long missions to Mars, with fresh foods providing nutrition, mood benefits, antioxidants, and possible radiation-protection benefits. However, the article appears centered on eating lettuce grown in space rather than a dedicated explanation of supporting life on other planets.","learned":"Growing crops on spacecraft or other planets is a proposed way to help astronauts sustain themselves during long missions and on Mars, with benefits for food, mood, antioxidants, and possibly radiation protection."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nasa mars habitat (+ radiation shield shielding shelter) (+ astronauts humans) (+ life support oxygen water food))","reason":"Search for authoritative NASA documents on Mars habitats that combine life-support needs with radiation shielding and shelter, another core requirement for supporting human life on other planets."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nasa mars habitat (+ radiation shield shielding shelter) (+ astronauts humans) (+ life support oxygen water food))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1525551273#4_3180659029","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3726","rank":1,"snippet":"exposure even more—into a key zone of survivability. By choosing the least radiated physical feature and the least radiated location, Paris has magnified the radiation-dampening effect of both. But how do you measure the levels of radiation in and out of the Mars tubes? Well, that’s where Earth’s lava tubes come in. “To complement this investigation, moreover, 30 in-situ radiation monitoring experiments have been conducted at analog lava tubes located at Mojave, CA, El Malpais, NM, and Flagstaff, AZ,” Paris explains in his paper. By measuring the outside and “inside” (the lava tubes) radiation levels in these locations, Paris has developed an overall sense of how much the lava tube structure reduces exposure to that <cover>radiation. From there, translating to Mars involves some fairly straightforward cross-multiplying. The High Resolution Imaging Science Experiment (HiRISE) camera on NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter captured this image of Hellas Planitia.\" , \"start_char\": 2033.000000 , \"title\": \"Mars Habitat - Life on Mars | Astronauts </cover>Could Live in Lava Tubes\" , \"url\": \"https://www.popularmechanics.com/science/a32451579/astronauts-lava-tubes-mars-shelter/\" }"}}
