{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_12_1623927183#7_3237811999","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly addresses a major requirement for humans living on Mars—radiation mitigation—and cites NASA expertise, but it is a general article about Mars living challenges rather than a dedicated life-support or habitat-support document.","learned":"Because Mars has a thin atmosphere, humans living there would face hazardous solar and cosmic radiation, so any settlement must reduce exposure through shielding or other mitigation."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nasa \"life support systems\" \"oxygen generation and recovery\" \"carbon dioxide removal\" \"water management\")","reason":"Search within the exact NASA dedicated life-support page headings to retrieve more grade-3 segments, especially oxygen generation and CO2 removal details not yet fully captured."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nasa \"life support systems\" \"oxygen generation and recovery\" \"carbon dioxide removal\" \"water management\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#9_2973312003","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3729","rank":9,"snippet":"MONITORING ATMOSPHERE MANAGEMENT<cover>Oxygen Generation and Recovery Carbon Dioxide Removal Trace Contaminant and Particulate Control WATER MANAGEMENT ADDITIONAL RESOURCES \" , \"segment\": \"Developing technologies to increase oxygen recovery. Advancing technologies for high-pressure, high-purity oxygen generation for exploration vehicles. Carbon Dioxide Removal A majority of the breathing air for astronauts is recycled within the spacecraft or habitat, and a key part of this process is the removal of exhaled carbon dioxide. Carbon dioxide removal and associated air-drying development efforts are focused on improving the current technology on the space station as well as assessing and examining the viability of other emerging or alternate technologies. Specific tasks include: Performing test scenarios to optimize performance. Screening alternative sorbents (materials used to absorb or liquids or gases) to determine the best candidate for a deep space carbon dioxide removal system. Testing new sorbents as replacements for those currently used. Ground testing of alternative carbon dioxide removal and compression systems. Ground testing various solid/liquid/thermal ammonia derivatives as an alternative to current systems.\" , \"start_char\": 6003.000000 , \"title\": \"Life Support Systems | NASA</cover>\" , \"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#11_2973315338","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#0_2973295301","rank":2,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#5_2973304272","rank":3,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#1_2973296989","rank":4,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#13_2973318619","rank":5,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#10_2973313563","rank":6,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#2_2973298936","rank":7,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#4_2973302470","rank":8,"relevance":3}]}}
