{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#9_2973312003","relevance":3,"reason":"This segment is from NASA's dedicated 'Life Support Systems' page and directly explains oxygen generation/recovery and carbon dioxide removal, including recycling breathing air, removing exhaled CO2, developing high-purity oxygen generation, and testing sorbents and alternate CO2-removal systems for deep-space habitats.","learned":"Human habitats beyond Earth need atmosphere-management systems that generate and recover oxygen, recycle breathing air, remove exhaled carbon dioxide, dry air, and use reliable sorbents or other technologies for deep-space missions."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3729","reason":"The NASA life-support heading query is producing dedicated grade-3 evidence, especially on oxygen and carbon-dioxide management; continue to retrieve any remaining relevant segments."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3729"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388444863#12_2973317093","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3729","rank":10,"snippet":"MONITORING ATMOSPHERE MANAGEMENT<cover>Oxygen Generation and Recovery Carbon Dioxide Removal Trace Contaminant and Particulate Control WATER MANAGEMENT ADDITIONAL RESOURCES \" , \"segment\": \"Specific tasks include: Developing new technologies to control trace contamination. Testing technologies to monitor aerosols in spacecraft. Developing filtration technologies for particulate control to meet exploration requirements and reduce crew maintenance requirements. WATER MANAGEMENT A major goal of life support systems is the development of water recovery systems to support long duration human exploration in deep space. Wastewater management systems have been designed to recycle crew­member urine, wastewater, and humidity for reuse as clean water. By doing so, the system reduces the amount of water and consumables that would need to be launched from Earth Current ISS systems recover water at a rate of approximately 74 percent. For longer, further missions into deep space, this rate must be increased so that astronauts can journey for months without resupply missions from Earth. To achieve this, the LSS work is underway to: Develop technologies to achieve 98 percent water loop closure.\" , \"start_char\": 7857.000000 , \"title\": \"Life Support Systems | NASA</cover>\" , \""}}
