{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_960839854#2_1955516259","relevance":3,"reason":"This ESA page segment is directly about closed-loop life support for off-world explorers, explaining that astronauts can recycle urine and sweat through filters, bacteria in bioreactors, and chemical reactions to produce clean water, food, and oxygen, aiming for self-sufficiency.","learned":"ESA's MELiSSA-style closed-loop systems support life beyond Earth by recycling wastes into water, oxygen, and food, reducing dependence on Earth resupply."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nasa \"space radiation\" mars astronauts (+ shielding shelter habitat) (+ protect protection))","reason":"Look for authoritative evidence on radiation protection, a major requirement for supporting human life on Mars and other planets, complementing air, water, food, and waste recycling evidence already found."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nasa \"space radiation\" mars astronauts (+ shielding shelter habitat) (+ protect protection))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388983668#2_2974456274","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3732","rank":1,"snippet":"of NASA's mission to send humans to Mars. \"The Martian\" film highlights the radiation dangers that could occur on a round trip to Mars. While the mission in the film is fictional, NASA has already started working on the technology to enable an actual trip to Mars in the 2030s. In the film, the astronauts’ habitat on Mars shields them from radiation, and indeed, radiation shielding will be a crucial technology for the voyage. From better shielding to advanced biomedical countermeasures, <cover>NASA currently studies how to protect astronauts and electronics from radiation – efforts that will have to be incorporated into every aspect of Mars mission planning, from spacecraft and habitat design to spacewalk protocols. “The space radiation </cover>environment will be a critical consideration for everything in the astronauts’ daily lives, both on the journeys between Earth and Mars and on the surface,” said Ruthan Lewis, an architect and engineer with the human spaceflight program at NASA's Goddard Space Flight Center in Greenbelt, Maryland. “ You’re constantly being bombarded by some amount of radiation.” Radiation, at its most basic, is simply waves or sub-atomic particles that transports energy to another entity – whether it is an astronaut or spacecraft "}}
