{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388983668#2_2974456274","relevance":3,"reason":"This NASA result is directly about protecting astronauts on Mars missions from space radiation, explaining that radiation shielding and biomedical countermeasures must be incorporated into spacecraft, habitat design, and spacewalk protocols to make human Mars exploration survivable.","learned":"Supporting human life on Mars requires radiation protection through shielding, habitat and spacecraft design, biomedical countermeasures, and operational protocols because astronauts are constantly exposed during transit and on the surface."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3732","reason":"The NASA radiation-protection query produced strong direct evidence for a major off-Earth life-support requirement; continue to find more authoritative results on shielding, habitat design, and radiation countermeasures."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3732"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388983668#11_2974474286","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3732","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_1388983668#11_2974474286\" , \"end_char\": 9618.000000 , \"headings\": \"Real Martians: How to Protect Astronauts from Space Radiation on Mars Moon to Mars Sep 30, 2015 Real Martians: How to <cover>Protect Astronauts from Space Radiation on Mars \" , \"segment\": \"NASA is currently investigating a handful of possibilities that could be used in anything from the spacecraft to the Martian habitat </cover>to space suits. “The best way to stop particle radiation is by running that energetic particle into something that’s a similar size,” said Pellish. “ Otherwise, it can be like you’re bouncing a tricycle off a tractor-trailer.” Because protons and neutrons are similar in size, one element blocks both extremely well—hydrogen, which most commonly exists as just a single proton and an electron. Conveniently, hydrogen is the most abundant element in the universe, and makes up substantial parts of some common compounds, such as water and plastics like polyethylene. Engineers could take advantage of already-required mass by processing the astronauts’ trash into plastic-filled tiles used to bolster radiation "}}
