{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nasa mars \"in situ resource utilization\" (+ water oxygen fuel materials) (+ astronauts humans habitat))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_651548698#5_1384092139","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3733","rank":1,"snippet":"Board? Mars Oxygen In-Situ Resource Utilization Experiment (MOXIE) The Radar Imager for Mars' Subsurface Experiment (RIMFAX) Planetary Instrument for X-ray Lithochemistry (PIXL) Mastcam-Z Mars Environmental Dynamics Analyzer (MEDA) Recommended by Our Editors Mars Helicopter 7 Minutes of Terror How to Watch the Perseverance Mars Rover Landing Get Our Best Stories! About Eric Griffith About Chloe Albanesius Read the latest from Chloe Albanesius \" , \"segment\": \"Advertisement Mars Oxygen In-Situ Resource Utilization Experiment (MOXIE) MOXIE (Credit: <cover>NASA/JPL-Caltech) Perseverance is carrying technology called the Mars Oxygen In-Situ Resource Utilization Experiment ( MOXIE ), which will generate O2 out of Mars' CO2 atmosphere—the kind of experiment that may help humans </cover>survive there someday. The 37.7-pound device \"breathes like a tree,\" NASA explains, inhaling carbon dioxide and exhaling oxygen. Given that oxygen makes up only 0.13% of the gas in Mars' atmosphere, compared to 21% in Earth's atmosphere, producing oxygen from the Martian atmosphere will be crucial for human survival on the Red Planet. MOXIE can also be used to create propellant, so people can explore Mars, and leave it to return to Earth. \" When we send humans to Mars, "}}
