{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_1409066633#8_2370234544","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage supports one aspect of sustaining humans on Mars—using local resources or 'living off the land' to make propellant instead of bringing everything from Earth—but it is mainly about mission fuel/power logistics rather than a dedicated explanation of how to support life on other planets.","learned":"Mars missions may reduce Earth-supplied mass by producing propellants locally, but doing so requires substantial power infrastructure such as large solar arrays, batteries, or fuel cells."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nasa mars \"water ice\" (+ astronauts humans) (+ oxygen fuel drinking habitat))","reason":"Search for authoritative evidence on using Martian water ice as a local resource for drinking water, oxygen, fuel, and habitat support, likely to produce direct grade-3 support for human survival on another planet."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ nasa mars \"water ice\" (+ astronauts humans) (+ oxygen fuel drinking habitat))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_52_1483058356#11_3012973026","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3735","rank":1,"snippet":"the University of Alberta in Canada, a Mars 2020 returned-sample scientist, said during the July 28 news conference. Testing out new exploration tech Click here for more Space.com videos... Though Mars 2020 is primarily a science mission, it will also test out a variety of technologies that may pave the way for ambitious exploration efforts in the future. For example, one of Perseverance's seven instruments, the <cover>Mars Oxygen In-Situ Resource Utilization Experiment ( MOXIE ), will generate oxygen from the Red Planet's thin, carbon dioxide-dominated atmosphere. Such gear, if scaled up, could help astronauts explore the Martian surface, a goal that NASA wants to achieve in the 2030s. The rover also carries a ground-penetrating radar instrument that will be able to detect subsurface water ice, </cover>another resource that human pioneers could exploit on the Red Planet. And Perseverance has a traveling companion, a 4-lb. ( 1.8 kilograms) helicopter called Ingenuity that will make the journey to Mars on the rover's belly. After Perseverance rolls upon a suitable Martian airfield, Ingenuity will deploy and attempt a few short flights — the first ever by a rotorcraft on a world beyond Earth. \"Ingenuity is going to "}}
