{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"closed-loop life support\" (+ mars moon planet planets spacecraft) (+ oxygen water food waste) (+ humans astronauts))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_36204385#1_65330854","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3737","rank":1,"snippet":": 1478.000000 , \"headings\": \"Living on Mars - Making Clean Water on Mars Living on Mars - Making Clean Water on Mars Living on Mars - The Water We Drink How Much Water Do We Need? 1. Bring the Water With Us Making Clean Water on Mars from Hydrazine Making Water on Mars from CO 2 2. Water from Mars Extracting the Water on Mars from Soil The Martian Ice Caps 3. Recycling Water with <cover>Closed-Loop Life Support Conclusion Previously on 'Living on Mars' - The Mars Oxygenator \" , \"segment\": \"​Unfortunately - no. The water on Mars is so full of salts, called perchlorates, that it wold be highly toxic to humans </cover>and plants. Perchlorates are so toxic, in fact, that several states in the U.S., including California and Massachusetts, limit the amount of perchlorates that can be in your water. ​Think of it this way: The water on Mars is saltier than the saltiest water on Earth: Don Juan Pond -- in the heart of Antarctica's Wright Valley. As NASA planetologist Chris Mckay stated: Nothing can live in the brine of Don Juan Pond. ​So what does that mean for "}}
